-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- #build-loop: a process that runs forever
-- #build-loop init step copy copy-id "file": init : St, step : St → Step W St, copy : St → St and
-- copy-id : (s : St) → copy s ≡ s, St a first-order type holding no byte strings. The binary runs
-- step from init; when a round ends (sdone s') the next begins from s'. Memory: the arena is two
-- halves; at each round the state is copied into the other half by copy, compiled like any
-- definition (it rebuilds the value's cells, so its result lives in the half being entered), and
-- everything the last round allocated is free again. copy-id is why this changes no answer: the
-- state the next round sees is the state. w-alloc memory is its own mapping and is never reclaimed.
loop-step-ty : Type → Type
loop-step-ty St = St → Step W St
